{"title":"Obtuse triangle","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>An <b>obtuse triangle</b> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangle\" title=\"Triangle\">triangle</a> in which one <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Angle\" title=\"Angle\">angle</a> is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Obtuse_angle\" title=\"Obtuse angle\">obtuse angle</a>.  Any triangle which is not obtuse is either a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Right_triangle\" title=\"Right triangle\">right triangle</a> or an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Acute_triangle\" title=\"Acute triangle\">acute triangle</a>.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/8/7a8842c5da6df412c69e32092fff83de88d03c4d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(1cm); pair A, B, C, D, E, F;  A = (0, 0); B = (2, 0); C = (-1, 4);  draw(A--B--C--cycle); draw(anglemark(B, A, C, 10)); label(&quot;$100^\\circ$&quot;, A, 3NE);  D = (3, 0); E = (7, 0); F = (5, 2);  draw(D--E--F--cycle); draw(anglemark(D, F, E, 10)); label(&quot;$140^\\circ$&quot;, F, 4S); [/asy]\" width=\"382\" height=\"192\" />\n</p><p>Obtuse triangles only have one obtuse angle because the angles of a triangle must sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/4/c4460b5100c3ce982ab5ebb20a8c3871e9cfdc54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. Two obtuse angles would sum to more than that.\n</p><p>Obtuse triangles can be defined in other ways: \n</p>\n<ul><li>A triangle is obtuse if and only if two of its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Altitude\" title=\"Altitude\">altitudes</a> lie entirely outside the triangle.  (There is no triangle with exactly one altitude or all three altitudes outside the triangle.)</li>\n<li>A triangle with sides of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/3/b0391e714bcd74ff75bc89954b855cddc7ce7ab2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c &gt; a, b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />, is obtuse if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/3/3b3a220a05502dd345b62bb1ab1f40cd67c8c275.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2 + b^2 &lt; c^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"16\" />.  This is known as the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometric_inequality\" title=\"Geometric inequality\"> Pythagorean Inequality</a>.  It follows directly from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Law_of_Cosines\" title=\"Law of Cosines\">Law of Cosines</a>.</li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Obtuse_triangle&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018003009\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.012 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.022 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 28/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 286/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1518/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.229      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    6.229      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3827:|#|:idhash:c